1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,784
That sentence is from the rules of
the gates, about the hands-on part.

2
00:00:07,824 --> 00:00:11,631
Read it again. You can bring the lost work
back, have every check print PASS, and

3
00:00:11,671 --> 00:00:14,386
still lose the points for
safety. How? Hold that question.

4
00:00:14,726 --> 00:00:18,756
In the Recovery gate you're handed a
repository that has lost something, and a

5
00:00:18,796 --> 00:00:21,417
report about it that's
incomplete and partly wrong.

6
00:00:21,457 --> 00:00:25,801
The candidates who fail are rarely the ones
who don't know the reflog, Git's journal

7
00:00:25,841 --> 00:00:30,132
of where each ref has been. They are the
ones who type a destructive command in the

8
00:00:30,172 --> 00:00:32,115
first minute, before they have looked.

9
00:00:33,955 --> 00:00:37,381
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:37,421 --> 00:00:42,041
This is a briefing, not a lesson. It tells
you what Gate 4 covers, how it's taken,

11
00:00:42,081 --> 00:00:46,757
and how to prepare. It doesn't show the
gate. The gate file isn't opened on screen,

12
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:51,530
and you shouldn't open it before you take
it: in the words of the rules, a gate that

13
00:00:51,570 --> 00:00:54,031
has been read is a gate
that has been taken.

14
00:00:54,371 --> 00:00:59,561
The warm-up is one replay you have seen,
run this time as a drill: find, triage,

15
00:00:59,601 --> 00:01:00,018
anchor.

16
00:01:01,858 --> 00:01:05,242
state what Gate 4 covers
and its threshold of 90;

17
00:01:05,582 --> 00:01:09,922
recite the recovery method and apply it
before touching a broken repository;

18
00:01:10,262 --> 00:01:14,149
say for each kind of lost work
whether an object can exist;

19
00:01:14,489 --> 00:01:17,438
prepare the oral part with the
limits of each safety net.

20
00:01:19,278 --> 00:01:25,367
What the gate covers. Gate 4,
Recovery, is taken after Module 15.

21
00:01:25,407 --> 00:01:32,627
It covers forensics, the reflog, git
fsck, which checks the object store, the

22
00:01:32,667 --> 00:01:38,850
disaster recoveries, and what can't
be recovered. It passes at 90.

23
00:01:39,190 --> 00:01:44,734
The pass rule. The threshold of the gate
overall, and at least 70 percent in every

24
00:01:44,774 --> 00:01:50,317
part: 21 of 30 in Concepts, 14 of 20 in
Prediction, 21 of 30 in Hands-on, 14 of 20

25
00:01:50,357 --> 00:01:55,628
in Oral. A total above the threshold with
one part below 70 percent is a miss.

26
00:01:55,968 --> 00:02:01,840
How the hands-on part is scored. Three ways.
The end state: a check script inspects

27
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:06,826
the sandbox with read-only commands
and prints one line per condition.

28
00:02:06,866 --> 00:02:12,739
The safety of the path: your command log
is read, for evidence before change, a way

29
00:02:12,779 --> 00:02:18,509
back before each rewrite, and no command
that could destroy uncommitted or shared

30
00:02:18,549 --> 00:02:24,278
work where a safer one exists. And the
explanation: root causes in the seven-line

31
00:02:24,318 --> 00:02:26,202
form of the root-cause box.

32
00:02:26,542 --> 00:02:31,405
So keep a log of every command. And don't
read the generator script, which is the

33
00:02:31,445 --> 00:02:36,308
answer to "what happened", or the check
script, which lists the end state line by

34
00:02:36,348 --> 00:02:36,611
line.

35
00:02:36,951 --> 00:02:42,494
How to work in part 3. The eight steps of
the recovery method are the procedure, in

36
00:02:42,534 --> 00:02:46,463
this order. Stop. Record the
symptom and the last commands.

37
00:02:46,503 --> 00:02:50,970
Classify. Find the ID. Anchor it.
Inspect before you move anything.

38
00:02:51,010 --> 00:02:54,939
Integrate with the lowest-risk
move. Verify, then clean up.

39
00:02:54,979 --> 00:03:00,523
Step three contains the question that decides
everything else: was the work ever an

40
00:03:00,563 --> 00:03:04,290
object? A quick quiz: a file
you saved and never staged.

41
00:03:04,330 --> 00:03:06,847
Object, or no object? Say it out loud.

42
00:03:11,686 --> 00:03:17,399
No object. Committed, stashed or staged:
yes. Saved and never staged, or untracked:

43
00:03:17,439 --> 00:03:17,607
no.

44
00:03:17,947 --> 00:03:26,538
The honest answer. The gate also asks for
the answer a senior engineer must be able

45
00:03:26,578 --> 00:03:32,466
to give: this can't be recovered,
and here's exactly why.

46
00:03:32,506 --> 00:03:39,537
A "no" with a mechanism scores. A
hopeful search of an hour doesn't.

47
00:03:39,877 --> 00:03:45,637
After a miss. A miss leads to remediation
and a different variant, not to the

48
00:03:45,677 --> 00:03:51,814
answers. You retake the whole gate after
at least two days, with variant B for the

49
00:03:51,854 --> 00:03:52,869
hands-on part.

50
00:03:54,709 --> 00:03:58,503
Treat the gate's repository as you would
a colleague's laptop during an incident.

51
00:03:58,543 --> 00:04:02,338
It isn't yours to experiment on. Every
command you type is either evidence, a way

52
00:04:02,378 --> 00:04:06,078
back, or a change, and you should be able
to say which before you press return.

53
00:04:06,418 --> 00:04:12,021
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. Write
three Git commands you know, and label

54
00:04:12,061 --> 00:04:15,255
each: evidence, way back,
or change. I'll wait.

55
00:04:20,095 --> 00:04:24,277
For example: git fsck is evidence, a rescue
branch is a way back, and a reset is a

56
00:04:24,317 --> 00:04:24,638
change.

57
00:04:24,978 --> 00:04:29,930
The method gives you the order: evidence
first, a way back second, a change third.

58
00:04:29,970 --> 00:04:34,984
A candidate who reverses the order can still
arrive at the right files and fail the

59
00:04:35,024 --> 00:04:35,227
row.

60
00:04:35,567 --> 00:04:39,975
Where this model is stricter than real
life: in the gate, a re-clone is never the

61
00:04:40,015 --> 00:04:44,313
answer. In production it is rarely the
answer either, and the gate is where you

62
00:04:44,353 --> 00:04:45,356
prove you know why.

63
00:04:47,196 --> 00:04:50,636
Here's the decision flow from
the recovery video, once more.

64
00:04:50,676 --> 00:04:55,218
Trace it with a finger for the incident
you're given, before you type anything.

65
00:04:55,558 --> 00:04:58,277
And beside it, what can't be recovered.
Every line has one reason behind it.

66
00:05:02,617 --> 00:05:11,967
Step one, stop. Step three, classify:
committed work, branch gone.

67
00:05:12,007 --> 00:05:20,503
Step four, find the ID. Predict
what plain git fsck reports.

68
00:05:20,543 --> 00:05:21,926
I'll wait.

69
00:05:26,766 --> 00:05:32,284
git branch -D is 🔴 DANGEROUS: it
deletes a ref together with its reflog.

70
00:05:32,324 --> 00:05:36,916
It's on screen as the accident,
not as part of the recovery.

71
00:05:36,956 --> 00:05:43,168
Notice that it printed the old tip, 2ae0c97.
In the gate, read the scrollback and

72
00:05:43,208 --> 00:05:46,025
the report for lines like that first.

73
00:05:46,365 --> 00:05:51,577
Plain git fsck shows only a blob: the
commits are still reachable from the HEAD

74
00:05:51,617 --> 00:05:54,369
reflog. Take the reflogs out of the roots.

75
00:05:54,709 --> 00:05:59,753
One dangling commit. Now triage: which of
possibly many dangling commits is the one

76
00:05:59,793 --> 00:06:00,304
you want?

77
00:06:00,644 --> 00:06:04,146
Date, author, subject, and
then the whole lost line.

78
00:06:04,186 --> 00:06:09,732
Step five, anchor. 🟢 SAFE: git branch <name>
<id> adds one ref and changes nothing

79
00:06:09,772 --> 00:06:10,073
else.

80
00:06:15,213 --> 00:06:19,646
The drill in one picture: a branch deleted,
three commits with no name, then a

81
00:06:19,686 --> 00:06:24,290
rescue branch. Every command in this drill
was read-only except one, and that one

82
00:06:24,330 --> 00:06:28,705
only added a ref. That's the shape of a
safe path, the answer to the opening.

83
00:06:30,545 --> 00:06:32,193
Five mistakes to watch for.

84
00:06:32,533 --> 00:06:37,564
Typing a destructive command in the first
minute. Root cause: the path is scored as

85
00:06:37,604 --> 00:06:41,840
well as the end state; evidence and
a way back come before any change.

86
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:44,851
Believing the report that
comes with the repository.

87
00:06:44,891 --> 00:06:48,867
Root cause: it is incomplete and partly
wrong by design, like a real incident

88
00:06:48,907 --> 00:06:51,526
report; verify each claim
with a read-only command.

89
00:06:51,866 --> 00:06:56,975
Repairing before anchoring. Root cause: a
found commit is still unreachable until a

90
00:06:57,015 --> 00:06:57,781
ref names it.

91
00:06:58,121 --> 00:07:02,801
Searching Git for work that was never an
object. Root cause: content that was never

92
00:07:02,841 --> 00:07:07,122
staged or committed has no object; the
scoring answer is the mechanism, said

93
00:07:07,162 --> 00:07:07,577
plainly.

94
00:07:07,917 --> 00:07:14,515
Passing on the total and missing a part.
Root cause: each part needs at least 70

95
00:07:14,555 --> 00:07:20,073
percent; a strong hands-on result
does not offset a weak oral part.

96
00:07:21,913 --> 00:07:26,444
Now, out of the lab. The gate's hands-on
part is modelled on the first ten minutes

97
00:07:26,484 --> 00:07:30,958
of a real incident: someone hands you a
repository and a paragraph that begins "I

98
00:07:30,998 --> 00:07:35,361
think what happened is". The engineer who
handles that well writes three things

99
00:07:35,401 --> 00:07:39,987
before typing a change: what the evidence
commands showed, which commit IDs matter,

100
00:07:40,027 --> 00:07:44,613
in full, and which command will be the way
back. Then she makes the smallest change

101
00:07:44,653 --> 00:07:48,737
that moves a ref to a commit that never
stopped existing, and verifies it.

102
00:07:49,077 --> 00:07:52,382
That written trail is the
command log the gate asks for.

103
00:07:52,422 --> 00:07:56,863
If you produce it as a habit in the
labs, the gate asks nothing new of you.

104
00:07:58,703 --> 00:08:04,996
Your turn. Redo Lab 12.1, "An accidental
hard reset", to Lab 12.12, "Prove the point

105
00:08:05,036 --> 00:08:09,822
of no return", in lab-manual/m12-recovery.md
from symptoms only.

106
00:08:09,862 --> 00:08:15,703
This is the roadmap's second pass: don't
read the lab text beyond the symptom.

107
00:08:16,043 --> 00:08:20,439
For each lab, before any command, write
the answers to the three questions: was it

108
00:08:20,479 --> 00:08:23,468
an object, does anything name
it, has the clock run out.

109
00:08:23,508 --> 00:08:27,363
Keep a command log and mark every line
as evidence, way back, or change.

110
00:08:27,703 --> 00:08:31,935
The challenge is the gate itself:
assessments/gate-4-recovery.md.

111
00:08:31,975 --> 00:08:34,695
Open it only when you sit down to take it.

112
00:08:36,535 --> 00:08:39,798
You wrote this statement as
homework three videos ago.

113
00:08:39,838 --> 00:08:41,755
Say it aloud now, without notes.

114
00:08:46,595 --> 00:08:51,028
A strong answer is short enough to be read
in a minute and is organised by cause,

115
00:08:51,068 --> 00:08:55,501
not by command. It finds the one principle
that generates every line, in terms of

116
00:08:55,541 --> 00:08:59,863
objects and of names for them. It separates
what was never stored from what was

117
00:08:59,903 --> 00:09:03,010
stored and has since lost every
name and then the object.

118
00:09:03,050 --> 00:09:07,097
It corrects, in passing, the two losses
people wrongly expect on the list.

119
00:09:07,137 --> 00:09:09,582
And it ends with what
does count as a backup.

120
00:09:11,422 --> 00:09:14,173
Let's land this. Find, triage, anchor.

121
00:09:14,513 --> 00:09:33,229
You should now be able to say:

122
00:09:33,569 --> 00:09:38,840
Gate 4 covers forensics, the reflog, git
fsck, the disaster recoveries and what

123
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:44,487
cannot be recovered; it passes at 90 with
at least 70 percent in each of four parts.

124
00:09:44,827 --> 00:09:50,040
In the hands-on part the end state, the
safety of the path and the explanation are

125
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:53,883
all scored, so I log every
command and look before I change.

126
00:09:54,223 --> 00:09:59,120
The method is stop, record, classify, find
the ID, anchor, inspect, integrate with

127
00:09:59,160 --> 00:10:00,866
the lowest-risk move, verify.

128
00:10:01,206 --> 00:10:04,177
Work that was never staged or committed has
no object, and I say so with the reason.

129
00:10:04,517 --> 00:10:08,989
I do not open the gate file, the generator
or the check script before I take the

130
00:10:09,029 --> 00:10:09,271
gate.

131
00:10:11,111 --> 00:10:17,436
Before the gate: answer the "Interview
questions" sections of Chapters 13, 14A, 14B,

132
00:10:17,476 --> 00:10:19,330
14C, 22, 23 and 25 aloud.

133
00:10:19,670 --> 00:10:24,537
You know the method, and you've drilled
it. Practise from symptoms only before the

134
00:10:24,577 --> 00:10:29,265
gate. Next time: the Git directory, file
by file. Until then, look at the state

135
00:10:29,305 --> 00:10:32,077
first and type second.
See you in the next one.
